{"chunk_id": "380b36ff4c84", "text": "Bhishma continues instructing Yudhishthira on the duties and realities of kingship. A ruler must combine diligence, intelligence, surveillance, and strategic deception, never underestimating enemies and always prioritizing exertion and preparedness. Bhishma stresses that effective rule requires both candour and calculated cunning, because kingdoms are coveted and vulnerable. Yudhishthira then asks a deeper question: why one man, the king, rules over many equals. Bhishma answers with a mythic history. In the earliest age, people governed themselves righteously without kings, but moral decline\u2014greed, lust, anger, and confusion\u2014destroyed order and even caused the Vedas to disappear. The gods appealed to Brahma, who created the science of governance, Dandaniti, the doctrine of rule through law, discipline, and punishment, establishing the philosophical foundation of kingship.\nBhishma explains the origin of kingship, the decline of early human virtue, and the creation of political science and the first righteous ruler, Prithu.\nThemes: origin of kingship, decline of virtue, dandaniti political science, divine legitimacy of rulers, law and punishment", "vector": [0.011688574217259884, -0.007908880710601807, 0.006404121872037649, -0.02006344497203827, -0.021836794912815094, 0.02736395038664341, -0.01660735532641411, -0.007067509926855564, -0.019092632457613945, -0.021086033433675766, -0.002095335628837347, -0.011371443048119545, 0.017176898196339607, 0.017306338995695114, -0.02417968586087227, 0.005608056206256151, 0.05242383852601051, -0.0026066298596560955, 0.034172575920820236, 0.003067765384912491, -0.0015791872283443809, -0.01809593290090561, 0.005510975141078234, 0.009604564867913723, -0.013358370400965214, 0.008400758728384972, 0.03368069976568222, -0.01583070494234562, 0.00889910850673914, -0.018536033108830452, -0.003247365588322282, -0.004323348868638277, -0.0058183991350233555, -0.01092486921697855, -0.02915024384